@*
 * FILE ROLE: Demonstrates opting out of the shared layout with Layout = null.
 *            The view renders ONLY its own markup — no outer HTML shell.
 * SECTIONS IN THIS FILE:
 *   1. Layout = null — opting out of the layout
 *   2. When to use Layout = null
 *@

@*
 * SECTION 1: Layout = null — OPTING OUT OF THE LAYOUT
 *
 * _ViewStart.cshtml runs before this view and sets Layout = "_Layout".
 * Setting Layout = null in THIS view's @{ } block OVERRIDES that assignment.
 *
 * The Razor engine then renders only the markup below — no DOCTYPE, no <html>,
 * no nav, no footer.  The HTTP response body is exactly what you see below.
 *
 * Precedence order for Layout:
 *   1. Per-view @{ Layout = … }              ← highest priority (wins)
 *   2. Folder _ViewStart.cshtml
 *   3. Parent-folder _ViewStart.cshtml       ← lowest
 *
 * SECTION 2: WHEN TO USE Layout = null
 *
 *   - AJAX partial-page responses
 *       The controller returns a View() and the browser only wants a fragment,
 *       not the full page chrome.  Setting Layout = null avoids sending nav/footer.
 *
 *   - HTML email templates
 *       Emails need their own DOCTYPE/table-based layout; the site chrome would break them.
 *
 *   - PDF / print generation
 *       Libraries like IronPDF or SelectPdf render a Razor view to PDF;
 *       the site chrome is unnecessary and wasteful.
 *
 *   - Integration / snapshot tests on raw view output
 *       Asserting on a specific HTML fragment is easier without the outer shell.
 *
 *   - Embedded iframe content
 *       A view shown inside an <iframe> should not repeat the outer shell.
 *@
@{
    Layout = null;      // _ViewStart set "_Layout"; this line overrides it to nothing
}

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>No-Layout Demo</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>No-Layout View</h1>
    <p>
        This page was rendered by <code>Views/Page/NoLayout.cshtml</code> with
        <code>Layout = null</code>.  There is no shared nav bar, footer, or
        outer HTML from <code>_Layout.cshtml</code>.  This is the complete
        HTTP response body.
    </p>
    <p>
        Common use cases: AJAX fragment responses, email templates,
        PDF generation, embedded iframes, integration test assertions.
    </p>
    <a href="/">Back to Index (uses _Layout.cshtml)</a>
</body>
</html>
